1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,600
Imagine your car breaks down tomorrow,
and the repair costs R10,000.

2
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,840
Could you pay for it from your savings?

3
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:13,120
In a survey by JustMoney this year,
with more than 5,400 South Africans,

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:15,920
fewer than one in five said they could.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,640
That's what an emergency fund fixes.

6
00:00:18,680 --> 00:00:23,120
In this video, you'll learn what it is,
exactly how big yours should be,

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,920
where to keep it so it's
safe and earns interest,

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,040
and how to start with just R200 a month.

9
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,800
Let's start from zero.

10
00:00:30,920 --> 00:00:33,520
So, what is an emergency fund?

11
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:38,240
It's money you put aside for one
job only: paying for surprises.

12
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:40,480
Not wants. Surprises.

13
00:00:40,520 --> 00:00:43,920
It's not savings for a goal,
and it's not an investment.

14
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,840
Its job is to be there,
in cash, on your worst day.

15
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:51,947
A real emergency is something you
didn't plan, that you can't ignore,

16
00:00:51,973 --> 00:00:54,560
and that can't wait. Losing your job.

17
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:58,613
A medical bill. The car breaking down,
when you need it to get to work.

18
00:00:58,667 --> 00:01:01,600
The geyser bursting.
A funeral in the family.

19
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:03,480
What isn't an emergency?

20
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:05,253
A holiday. A new phone.

21
00:01:05,307 --> 00:01:10,400
A Black Friday deal. Those are good things
to save for too, but in a different pot.

22
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:13,200
Think of your emergency
fund as a spare tyre.

23
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:14,760
You hope you never need it.

24
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:17,560
But when you do, you're
very glad it's there.

25
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,440
Why does it matter so much?

26
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:23,333
Because when a surprise comes and
there's no money, people borrow.

27
00:01:23,387 --> 00:01:27,653
A loan, a credit card, a store card,
or money from a loan shark.

28
00:01:27,707 --> 00:01:32,600
And now the surprise costs you interest,
every month, long after it's over.

29
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:34,773
And debt has a way of growing.

30
00:01:34,827 --> 00:01:38,760
One loan to pay for the surprise,
then another loan to pay the first one.

31
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,240
Or people take money
from their retirement.

32
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:43,000
Remember the two-pot system?

33
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:48,120
Say you earn R25,000 a month and you
take R10,000 from your savings pot.

34
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:53,160
At a 26% tax rate, SARS
takes about R2,600 first,

35
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:55,467
and your fund charges a fee on top.

36
00:01:55,493 --> 00:01:58,000
With an emergency fund, you just pay.

37
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:00,760
No interest, no tax, no stress.

38
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:02,440
Then you fill it up again.

39
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,680
So, how big should yours be?

40
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:09,013
The common rule of thumb is three to
six months of your essential expenses.

41
00:02:09,067 --> 00:02:11,280
Not your salary. Your essentials.

42
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:14,133
Rent or bond. Food. Transport.

43
00:02:14,267 --> 00:02:15,520
Electricity and water.

44
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:17,733
Airtime and data. School fees.

45
00:02:17,787 --> 00:02:20,040
And the minimum payments on any debt.

46
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:21,307
To find your number,

47
00:02:21,413 --> 00:02:25,400
take three months of bank statements
and add up only the things you must pay,

48
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:28,133
even in a bad month. Let's take Thandi.

49
00:02:28,187 --> 00:02:31,080
Her essentials add up to R10,000 a month.

50
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:33,760
So three months is R30,000.

51
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:35,840
Six months is R60,000.

52
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:38,320
If that sounds impossible, don't worry.

53
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:42,000
Nobody starts there.
Build it in three levels.

54
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:45,920
Level one: a starter fund of R2,000.

55
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,400
That covers most small surprises.

56
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:51,920
Level two: one month of essentials.

57
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:54,480
For Thandi, that's R10,000.

58
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:57,520
Level three: three to six months.

59
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,880
Aim for six if your income goes up and
down, like freelance or commission work,

60
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:04,560
or if you support other people.

61
00:03:05,120 --> 00:03:08,160
Three can be enough if
your job is very stable.

62
00:03:09,038 --> 00:03:11,438
Next, where should you keep it?

63
00:03:11,518 --> 00:03:14,318
Your emergency fund needs three things.

64
00:03:14,398 --> 00:03:18,878
It must be safe. It must be
easy to reach within a few days.

65
00:03:18,958 --> 00:03:23,278
And it should earn some interest,
so inflation doesn't eat it.

66
00:03:23,358 --> 00:03:26,718
Inflation was 4.5% in September.

67
00:03:26,798 --> 00:03:30,478
The simplest home is a separate
savings account at a bank.

68
00:03:30,598 --> 00:03:34,078
Not your everyday account,
where it's too easy to spend.

69
00:03:34,158 --> 00:03:36,318
Once it grows, you can split it.

70
00:03:36,398 --> 00:03:38,798
Keep one month in instant access.

71
00:03:38,878 --> 00:03:42,478
Put the rest in a notice account,
where you give the bank notice,

72
00:03:42,558 --> 00:03:46,638
for example 32 days,
and usually earn a bit more.

73
00:03:46,838 --> 00:03:49,278
Avoid locking all of
it in a fixed deposit,

74
00:03:49,358 --> 00:03:51,998
because then you can't
get it in an emergency.

75
00:03:52,078 --> 00:03:55,278
The good news: bank
deposits are protected.

76
00:03:55,398 --> 00:03:57,198
Since April 2024,

77
00:03:57,278 --> 00:04:02,798
the Corporation for Deposit Insurance
covers up to R100,000 per person at each

78
00:04:02,878 --> 00:04:05,838
bank, for free, if a bank fails.

79
00:04:05,878 --> 00:04:09,838
That includes savings, notice
and fixed deposit accounts.

80
00:04:09,918 --> 00:04:14,398
But here's a detail. A money market
account at a bank is a bank deposit,

81
00:04:14,478 --> 00:04:19,758
so it's covered. A money market unit
trust is an investment, so it isn't.

82
00:04:19,838 --> 00:04:23,251
It's usually low risk,
but it's not guaranteed.

83
00:04:23,305 --> 00:04:26,278
And interest is good news at tax time too.

84
00:04:26,318 --> 00:04:27,878
If you're under 65,

85
00:04:27,918 --> 00:04:33,385
the first R23,800 of interest
you earn each year is tax-free.

86
00:04:33,491 --> 00:04:34,998
Where shouldn't you keep it?

87
00:04:35,038 --> 00:04:38,758
Under the mattress, where it
can be stolen and earns nothing.

88
00:04:38,798 --> 00:04:42,185
In shares, which can fall
just when you need the money.

89
00:04:42,291 --> 00:04:44,998
And not in your Tax-Free Savings Account.

90
00:04:45,038 --> 00:04:50,198
If you withdraw from a TFSA,
you lose that contribution space forever.

91
00:04:50,238 --> 00:04:52,198
Let's see how fast it grows.

92
00:04:52,238 --> 00:04:55,998
In this example, we'll say
the account pays 6% a year.

93
00:04:56,078 --> 00:04:59,038
That's only an illustration;
check your own bank's rate.

94
00:04:59,118 --> 00:05:01,758
Thandi starts with R200 a month.

95
00:05:01,838 --> 00:05:05,598
After 10 months, she has about R2,045.

96
00:05:05,678 --> 00:05:10,478
Level one, done. Then she
pushes it up to R1,000 a month.

97
00:05:10,558 --> 00:05:14,678
About 26 months later, she passes R30,000.

98
00:05:14,718 --> 00:05:20,038
So in roughly three years, from zero,
she has three months of essentials saved.

99
00:05:20,078 --> 00:05:25,971
And her R30,000 earns her about
R1,850 a year in interest.

100
00:05:26,025 --> 00:05:30,931
That's far below the R23,800
limit, so it's tax-free.

101
00:05:30,985 --> 00:05:32,985
The key is not the amount.

102
00:05:33,011 --> 00:05:35,558
It's starting, and never stopping.

103
00:05:35,598 --> 00:05:39,558
If Thandi had started a year
earlier, she'd be a year closer.

104
00:05:39,598 --> 00:05:41,598
That's why today matters.

105
00:05:42,567 --> 00:05:45,047
But where do you find the R200?

106
00:05:45,247 --> 00:05:47,687
First, pay yourself first.

107
00:05:47,767 --> 00:05:50,567
Set up a debit order for
the day after payday,

108
00:05:50,647 --> 00:05:53,367
so the money moves
before you can spend it.

109
00:05:53,447 --> 00:05:56,487
Second, look at three
months of bank statements.

110
00:05:56,607 --> 00:06:00,407
Find the small things that add up:
subscriptions you don't use,

111
00:06:00,567 --> 00:06:04,647
bank fees, takeaways,
airtime bought in small bundles.

112
00:06:04,847 --> 00:06:07,367
Third, save the surprises too.

113
00:06:07,407 --> 00:06:11,767
A bonus, a tax refund,
a stokvel payout, a birthday gift.

114
00:06:11,887 --> 00:06:14,247
Put part of it straight into the fund.

115
00:06:14,487 --> 00:06:18,647
Fourth, when you finish paying off a
debt, keep paying that same amount,

116
00:06:18,727 --> 00:06:20,967
but now into your emergency fund.

117
00:06:21,047 --> 00:06:23,287
You were already living
without that money.

118
00:06:23,327 --> 00:06:25,847
And with Black Friday
coming, one more idea.

119
00:06:26,167 --> 00:06:30,967
Before you buy anything on credit,
ask: is my emergency fund there yet?

120
00:06:31,783 --> 00:06:37,287
Now, the five traps. Trap one: using
it for things that aren't emergencies.

121
00:06:37,607 --> 00:06:39,047
Be honest with yourself.

122
00:06:39,367 --> 00:06:42,087
Trap two: keeping it in your main account.

123
00:06:42,327 --> 00:06:44,807
If you can see it every
day, you'll spend it.

124
00:06:45,127 --> 00:06:47,767
Trap three: chasing high returns.

125
00:06:48,007 --> 00:06:50,527
This money is for safety, not growth.

126
00:06:50,647 --> 00:06:52,247
Investing comes after.

127
00:06:52,567 --> 00:06:54,887
Trap four: not refilling it.

128
00:06:55,047 --> 00:06:58,727
If you use it, rebuild it before
you save for anything else.

129
00:06:59,047 --> 00:07:02,167
Trap five: waiting until
your debt is paid off.

130
00:07:02,487 --> 00:07:06,567
Build your starter R2,000 first,
even while you pay debt.

131
00:07:06,807 --> 00:07:09,767
Otherwise the next surprise
just adds more debt.

132
00:07:10,007 --> 00:07:12,367
Here's how to start, step by step.

133
00:07:12,567 --> 00:07:16,367
Step one. Add up your essential
expenses for one month.

134
00:07:16,567 --> 00:07:19,447
Step two. Open a separate savings account.

135
00:07:19,687 --> 00:07:24,007
Check the fees, because on a small
balance, fees can eat your interest.

136
00:07:24,087 --> 00:07:27,847
Step three. Set up a debit
order for the day after payday.

137
00:07:28,007 --> 00:07:30,647
Even R200. Step four.

138
00:07:30,727 --> 00:07:34,487
Name the account "Emergency fund",
so you remember what it's for.

139
00:07:34,887 --> 00:07:40,367
Step five. Reach R2,000, then one
month, then three to six months.

140
00:07:40,567 --> 00:07:45,327
Step six. Check it once a year,
or when your rent or expenses change.

141
00:07:45,527 --> 00:07:49,687
Let's recap. An emergency fund
is money for surprises only.

142
00:07:50,007 --> 00:07:55,207
Aim for three to six months of essential
expenses, but start with R2,000.

143
00:07:55,327 --> 00:08:00,407
Keep it in a separate bank account that
earns interest, not in shares or your TFSA.

144
00:08:00,647 --> 00:08:04,567
Bank deposits are covered
up to R100,000 per bank.

145
00:08:04,647 --> 00:08:09,127
And start today, with a debit
order, even if it's just R200.

146
00:08:09,207 --> 00:08:12,007
If this helped,
subscribe to Money From Zero.

147
00:08:12,087 --> 00:08:15,927
New Shorts every day, and a
full guide like this every week.

148
00:08:16,087 --> 00:08:17,367
See you in the next one.

